<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Aizu Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1601 CE – 1868 CE · capital Aizuwakamatsu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Tokugawa branch domain in northern Japan whose loyalty to the shogunate ended in the tragic siege of Wakamatsu castle in 1868.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the Uesugi were moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yonezawa">Yonezawa</a> in 1601, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aizu">Aizu</a> passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamo-hideyuki">Gamo Hideyuki</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kato-yoshiaki">Kato Yoshiaki</a>. In 1643 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoshina-masayuki">Hoshina Masayuki</a>, half-brother of Shogun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-iemitsu">Tokugawa Iemitsu</a>, with 230,000 koku; his descendants, later surnamed Matsudaira, held it until the end.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoshina-masayuki">Masayuki</a>'s house code bound his heirs to absolute loyalty to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aizu">Aizu</a> was known for strict <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> education at the Nisshinkan school, and for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lacquerware">lacquerware</a> and sake. In 1862 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsudaira-katamori">Matsudaira Katamori</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> protector, policing the capital with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinsengumi">Shinsengumi</a> and fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choshu-domain">Choshu</a> radicals.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Branded an enemy after 1868, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aizu">Aizu</a> was the target of the imperial army; the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ouetsu-reppan-domei">Ouetsu Reppan Domei</a> formed partly to defend it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsuruga-castle">Wakamatsu castle</a> fell after a month-long siege, during which young Byakkotai soldiers took their own lives. The clan was exiled to Tonami in the far north.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/aizu-domain.jpg" alt="Aizu Domain" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Fumihiko Ueno · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1_%C5%8Ctemachi,_Aizuwakamatsu-shi,_Fukushima-ken_965-0873,_Japan_-_panoramio_(3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1601 CE – 1868 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aizuwakamatsu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hoshina Masayuki, Matsudaira Katamori</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Japan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wakamatsu Domain, Aizu han</dd></dl></aside></div>

